#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f9bb39-d662-5b7f-b26c-4c6a27fffc10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290} {:answer/id #uuid "40369435-8306-5137-8429-a26ad39afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365291} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ede10b-ba4c-5e79-b731-b8038347c020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365292} {:answer/id #uuid "5a09cbc5-7125-5f56-932d-0251a7308a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365293} {:answer/id #uuid "07428674-2986-5965-9daa-705a8555f7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75f9bb39-d662-5b7f-b26c-4c6a27fffc10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e05aabc-648f-50bd-bc69-595d4091baa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecee2124-1bd8-5650-8e8b-4c51642487c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163553" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(6-6x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/61/61388466bb2d97b5080707e769ae862e633bfb0fe1d2b5f16c75daa14da76e53.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(6-6x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6-6x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-6x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $3>0$ | $0$ | $-6<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6-6x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (6-6x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=6x-3{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-3{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =6(0)-3(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-3{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =12x-6{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+12x+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+12x+8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(1)=\\sqrt{-6(1)^{2}+12(1)+8}=\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ebcf16d-17e2-521f-84af-8c80fc770e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365331} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a32aa-8b15-5397-a3a9-3d850bb58c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365332} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea1d31-74b4-5ddf-8bc9-1bd6d0bd85b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365333} {:answer/id #uuid "b4036230-717e-5222-ab98-3a9fdf09553e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365334} {:answer/id #uuid "8832e1f0-db88-5028-8c7f-f4ff514e3d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4036230-717e-5222-ab98-3a9fdf09553e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365334} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f1e0c06-6d19-533b-b17a-f929a1be5595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19e94881-dc11-5b6a-8172-59477ad89f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163565" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $(10+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=12\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has a velocity of $2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $4\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a967d26d7c139b5316c57b5546f47e52b30b2ff9e2d25620c79d05843cf5bfce.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(10+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-(10+kx).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-(10+kx).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int -(10+kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =-10(0)-\\frac{k(0)^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ C & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=72-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s$ when $x=4\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =72-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{15})^{2} & =72-10(4)-\\frac{k(4)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 30 & =72-40-8k \\\\ 8k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
